p.src:
A translation in one coordinate has $\delta q=\epsilon.$ Here $F(q)=1$ in Eq. , so the corresponding conserved quantity is $p$. The generator claim predicts $\{f(q),p\}=\frac{df}{dq}.$ The lecture proves this first for powers by induction. The base case is $\{q,p\}=1$. Suppose $\{q^{n-1},p\}=(n-1)q^{n-2}.$ Then the product rule gives $\begin{aligned}
  \{q^n,p\}
  &=\{q\,q^{n-1},p\}\\
  &=q\{q^{n-1},p\}+\{q,p\}q^{n-1}\\
  &=(n-1)q^{n-1}+q^{n-1}\\
  &=nq^{n-1}.
  \end{aligned}$ Linearity extends the result to polynomials and to convergent power-series representations. More directly, the definition of the bracket gives Eq.  for every differentiable $f(q)$: $\{f(q),p\}
  =
  \frac{\partial f}{\partial q}
  \frac{\partial p}{\partial p}
  =f'(q).$

